Trustworthiness isn’t just accuracy

Suppose a model is 80% accurate.

That doesn’t tell the user whether this particular answer belongs to the 80% or the 20%.

A trustworthy system ideally provides signals that help distinguish:

“I have strong evidence for this prediction”

from

“This is my best answer, but several alternatives are plausible.”

Predictive uncertainty is one such signal.

If explanations are shown to users as transparency mechanisms, but explanation language doesn’t track predictive uncertainty, then the explanation may provide false reassurance.

And false reassurance can be worse than providing no explanation.

