There are actually three kinds of trust here

This distinction may help when explaining the work to your supervisor.

1. Trust in the answer

“Is the answer correct?”

Measured through correctness.

2. Trust in the model’s confidence

“Does the model know when its prediction is uncertain?”

Approached through EBSE and its relationship with correctness.

3. Trust in the explanation

“Does the explanation give the user an accurate signal about the uncertainty surrounding the prediction?”

This is what your EBSE ↔ GPT-perceived uncertainty experiment addresses.

And Experiment B adds:

“Does the explanation continue to advocate for the answer even when that answer is wrong?”

These are different properties.

A model can have useful predictive uncertainty while producing explanations that fail to communicate it.

