The most important distinction

Suppose Model A has low uncertainty and produces a confident explanation.

That’s not necessarily a problem.

Suppose Model B has high uncertainty and produces a cautious explanation.

Also sensible.

The trustworthiness failure occurs when:

Model uncertainty changes substantially, but the explanation presented to the human doesn’t meaningfully change with it.

Because then the explanation is not functioning as an informative trust signal.

It may still be a perfectly good rationale:

“Here are reasons supporting A.”

But it is not necessarily a good trustworthiness signal:

“Here is how strongly you should rely on A.”

That distinction could be central to your paper.

A strong one-line motivation

If your supervisor asks “Why should explanations reflect uncertainty?”, I would answer:

Because explanations influence whether humans trust and act on model outputs. If explanations remain confident when the model’s own prediction is unstable, they can conceal uncertainty and encourage inappropriate reliance. Therefore, a trustworthy explanation should not only justify what the model predicted; it should preserve enough information about predictive uncertainty for users to distinguish robust predictions from uncertain ones.

And then your research question follows naturally:

Do current LLM explanations actually provide that signal?

Your results so far suggest:

Only weakly and inconsistently — while remaining strongly capable of justifying incorrect answers.

That is a much stronger trustworthiness framing than simply saying “EBSE and explanation uncertainty have low correlation.”
